WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.094
Here's a question most people are too polite to ask.

2
00:00:06.094 --> 00:00:08.771
You run the same setup on a server twice.

3
00:00:08.771 --> 00:00:09.619
Is that safe?

4
00:00:09.619 --> 00:00:11.708
And why on earth wouldn't it be?

5
00:00:11.933 --> 00:00:14.628
Before computers, let's look after a house.

6
00:00:14.833 --> 00:00:19.106
Your neighbour goes away for a week and leaves you a list of chores.

7
00:00:19.106 --> 00:00:20.175
Water the plants.

8
00:00:20.175 --> 00:00:21.431
Turn the heating on.

9
00:00:21.431 --> 00:00:22.625
Lock the back door.

10
00:00:22.833 --> 00:00:26.209
On Monday you do every chore, top to bottom.

11
00:00:26.209 --> 00:00:26.900
Good job.

12
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:34.073
And every day after, you water the plants again, because the list says so.

13
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.435
By Friday, the pots are swimming.

14
00:00:36.435 --> 00:00:38.634
Those plants didn't need a sitter.

15
00:00:38.634 --> 00:00:40.187
They needed a lifeguard.

16
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.308
And notice, the trouble wasn't you.

17
00:00:42.308 --> 00:00:44.053
You followed the list perfectly.

18
00:00:44.053 --> 00:00:48.143
The list said what to do, when it should have said how the house should be.

19
00:00:49.167 --> 00:00:51.961
So let's rewrite the list as results.

20
00:00:51.961 --> 00:00:52.716
Soil damp.

21
00:00:52.716 --> 00:00:54.453
Room at twenty degrees.

22
00:00:54.453 --> 00:00:55.359
Door locked.

23
00:00:55.567 --> 00:00:58.438
Now each line starts with a look.

24
00:00:58.438 --> 00:00:59.917
Is the soil damp?

25
00:00:59.917 --> 00:01:01.918
Yes, so you do nothing.

26
00:01:01.918 --> 00:01:03.397
Is the room warm?

27
00:01:03.397 --> 00:01:03.745
Yes.

28
00:01:03.745 --> 00:01:05.399
Is the door locked?

29
00:01:05.399 --> 00:01:05.747
Yes.

30
00:01:05.967 --> 00:01:10.116
Run this list a hundred times and the house stays exactly right.

31
00:01:10.116 --> 00:01:11.932
Checking twice does no harm.

32
00:01:12.533 --> 00:01:15.847
Now say the wind blows the back door open on Wednesday.

33
00:01:16.067 --> 00:01:17.622
The same list notices.

34
00:01:17.622 --> 00:01:21.016
Soil fine, room fine, door open, so you lock it.

35
00:01:21.016 --> 00:01:25.047
One line reports changed, and only that one did any work.

36
00:01:26.267 --> 00:01:32.174
Ansible, the tool that sets up the inside of servers, works exactly like the second list.

37
00:01:32.174 --> 00:01:33.701
A playbook is the list.

38
00:01:33.701 --> 00:01:39.343
Each line is a task, and each task names a module, a small program that does one job.

39
00:01:39.567 --> 00:01:42.156
And each task is written as a result.

40
00:01:42.156 --> 00:01:45.725
name jq, state present, means jq must be installed.

41
00:01:45.725 --> 00:01:47.545
It doesn't say install it.

42
00:01:47.545 --> 00:01:49.855
It says how the server should be.

43
00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:50.848
Let's run it.

44
00:01:50.848 --> 00:01:55.413
The module looks at the server first, and acts only where something differs.

45
00:01:55.633 --> 00:01:57.547
First run, fresh server.

46
00:01:57.547 --> 00:01:59.141
Missing, so install.

47
00:01:59.141 --> 00:02:00.655
Old text, so write.

48
00:02:00.655 --> 00:02:02.090
Stopped, so start.

49
00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:04.243
Three tasks, three changed.

50
00:02:04.467 --> 00:02:05.943
Now the real question.

51
00:02:05.943 --> 00:02:10.371
We run the same playbook again, one minute later, nothing touched.

52
00:02:10.371 --> 00:02:11.847
How many tasks change?

53
00:02:11.847 --> 00:02:12.920
Pause and guess.

54
00:02:15.133 --> 00:02:15.522
Zero.

55
00:02:15.522 --> 00:02:20.651
Each module looks, finds the server already right, and reports ok.

56
00:02:21.467 --> 00:02:25.362
Here's the lab's real first playbook on its three practice servers.

57
00:02:25.362 --> 00:02:27.688
The first run changes things everywhere.

58
00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:31.695
The second run reports changed equals zero on every server.

59
00:02:31.695 --> 00:02:34.719
It took about ten seconds, and touched nothing.

60
00:02:34.933 --> 00:02:37.441
This property has a name: idempotence.

61
00:02:37.441 --> 00:02:39.882
An idempotent task is safe to repeat.

62
00:02:39.882 --> 00:02:44.105
Running it again, when nothing needs to change, changes nothing.

63
00:02:45.333 --> 00:02:47.952
Remember the door the wind blew open?

64
00:02:47.952 --> 00:02:49.792
On servers, it's a person.

65
00:02:49.792 --> 00:02:54.676
Someone logs in to web2 and adds a line to its login message by hand.

66
00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.278
Now the server and the code disagree.

67
00:02:58.278 --> 00:02:59.952
That gap is called drift.

68
00:03:00.167 --> 00:03:04.487
The next run looks, finds the extra line, and puts the file back.

69
00:03:04.487 --> 00:03:08.009
One task reports changed, exactly like the back door.

70
00:03:09.233 --> 00:03:10.267
So far so good.

71
00:03:10.267 --> 00:03:11.026
Now a trap.

72
00:03:11.026 --> 00:03:16.058
Ansible also has a shell module, which runs any command line you give it.

73
00:03:16.058 --> 00:03:19.436
Here's a task someone adds to a nightly playbook.

74
00:03:19.667 --> 00:03:22.198
It appends one line to a settings file.

75
00:03:22.198 --> 00:03:24.664
Ansible is idempotent, we just saw it.

76
00:03:24.664 --> 00:03:29.727
So after a week of nightly runs, how many copies of that line are in the file?

77
00:03:29.727 --> 00:03:30.765
Pause and guess.

78
00:03:32.967 --> 00:03:33.690
Well...

79
00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:34.311
seven.

80
00:03:34.311 --> 00:03:36.379
One for every night.

81
00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.832
The shell module can't look first.

82
00:03:38.832 --> 00:03:45.593
It has no idea what your command means, so it just runs it, every time, and reports changed every time.

83
00:03:45.593 --> 00:03:48.481
That's a chore, hiding in a list of results.

84
00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:52.374
Ansible's tasks are idempotent only when the module is.

85
00:03:52.374 --> 00:03:55.113
The tool can't make a chore safe for you.

86
00:03:55.333 --> 00:04:00.245
The fix is a module that knows the result: lineinfile, with the line you want.

87
00:04:00.245 --> 00:04:03.645
It checks whether the line is there, and adds it once.

88
00:04:04.867 --> 00:04:06.042
A few guardrails.

89
00:04:06.042 --> 00:04:08.600
Command and shell always say changed.

90
00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:10.813
So look for a real module first.

91
00:04:10.813 --> 00:04:16.413
ansible-doc minus l lists every module you have, and grep finds the one you need.

92
00:04:16.633 --> 00:04:19.943
And here's where our house sitter story stops working.

93
00:04:19.943 --> 00:04:24.662
A good sitter would notice a broken window that no line on the list mentions.

94
00:04:24.662 --> 00:04:25.827
Ansible never does.

95
00:04:26.033 --> 00:04:29.407
It keeps no state file and no memory of past runs.

96
00:04:29.407 --> 00:04:33.523
Delete the jq task from the playbook, and jq stays installed.

97
00:04:33.523 --> 00:04:38.448
To remove something, you write a task that says so, such as state absent.

98
00:04:39.667 --> 00:04:41.413
So the moral of the story.

99
00:04:41.413 --> 00:04:45.779
A chore repeated does harm, and a result checked twice does none.

100
00:04:45.779 --> 00:04:47.324
Write tasks as results.

101
00:04:47.533 --> 00:04:50.569
Every good module does the same three things.

102
00:04:50.569 --> 00:04:51.581
It looks first.

103
00:04:51.581 --> 00:04:54.145
It acts only where the server differs.

104
00:04:54.145 --> 00:04:56.776
And it tells you which: ok, or changed.

105
00:04:58.000 --> 00:04:59.076
A quick check.

106
00:04:59.076 --> 00:05:03.686
This task runs on a server where nginx is already installed.

107
00:05:03.686 --> 00:05:05.992
Does it report ok, or changed?

108
00:05:05.992 --> 00:05:07.221
Pause and think.

109
00:05:09.433 --> 00:05:09.993
Changed.

110
00:05:09.993 --> 00:05:11.183
Every single run.

111
00:05:11.183 --> 00:05:16.643
The shell module can't look first, so it never knows the job was already done.
